, "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 1st District, an R+6 seat in eastern Nebraska, features Republican Rep. Mike Flood seeking a third full term against Democrat Chris Backemeyer, with a Libertarian and an independent also on the ballot. Most rating outlets call the race Likely Republican, though a pair place it at Lean Republican and one at Safe Republican.\n\nFlood holds a clear financial edge, raising $2.7 million to Backemeyer's $1.0 million. Still, Backemeyer has posted unusually strong fundraising for a challenger, and a September Democratic-sponsored poll of likely voters showed the race tied at 43-42, a shift from spring surveys that had Flood ahead by 7-8 points. Betting markets give Backemeyer roughly a one-in-six chance. A favorable national environment for Democrats offsets much of the district's partisan lean and Flood's incumbency advantage, leaving the seat leaning Republican with real uncertainty about the final margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2177, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1689, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1196, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1777, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2315, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1394, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1156, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.114, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1142, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1121, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.173, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.1374, "margin": -6.6}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.1133, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.1024, "margin": -8.0}, {"